From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:12:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fpc1020: send INIT with the cmdbuf's length, not the
 envelope's

fpc1020_tz_send_locked() passed FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE (64) to
fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked() as the request length. That argument is the
CMDBUF's size, and the trustlet reads it: an INIT declaring 64 bytes is
accepted, answers status 0, and skips the sensor bring-up entirely.

Measured on taimen by varying the two candidate fields independently
through the ioctl path, which is otherwise identical, on an already-open
chardev:

  INIT len=64   word3=0      status=0      4ms
  INIT len=4096 word3=0      status=0    745ms
  INIT len=64   word3=4096   status=0      2ms
  INIT len=4096 word3=4096   status=0    754ms

Only the length matters; word 3 (the response-buffer-size field that
GET_FPC_INFO needs) makes no difference to INIT. A real INIT takes
~750ms of hardware bring-up. The short form returns success in 4ms
having done nothing.

The consequence was not a visible failure. The sensor stayed in reset,
so FP_INT_N stayed asserted from probe's own hw_reset; the line is armed
IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING, so once it is high no touch can produce another
edge. Meanwhile open() returned 0, ready went true, and fprintd
enumerated the device and started an enrolment against a sensor that had
never been initialised. On the device this was found on, the interrupt
had counted zero in seven hours of uptime.

After the fix, on a fresh boot: open() takes 895ms (cold trustlet load
plus a real INIT), gpio86 goes high -> low, and the interrupt counts 80
edges during bring-up. A capture loop then holds the 0x105 "no image
yet" baseline for 110 consecutive iterations with nobody touching.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/misc/fpc1020.c | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c b/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
index 0b4e14394bfe..329558629375 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
@@ -354,7 +354,17 @@ static int fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked(struct fpc1020 *fpc1020, void *cmdbuf,
 }
 
 /* Sends {group, cmd} with an otherwise-zeroed cmdbuf and no interest in
- * the response beyond the transport result -- used only for INIT. */
+ * the response beyond the transport result -- used only for INIT.
+ *
+ * The length passed to fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked() is the CMDBUF's size, not
+ * the envelope's, and getting it wrong is silent: an INIT declaring 64
+ * bytes is accepted by the trustlet, returns status 0, and skips the
+ * sensor bring-up altogether -- 4ms instead of the ~750ms a real INIT
+ * takes, with nothing logged and nothing to grep for. The sensor then
+ * never leaves reset: FP_INT_N stays asserted from probe's hw_reset, and
+ * since the line is armed IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING no touch can ever produce
+ * an edge, while open() succeeds and the whole stack up to fprintd looks
+ * healthy. Measured on taimen 2026-09-12 by varying this one field. */
 static int fpc1020_tz_send_locked(struct fpc1020 *fpc1020, u32 group, u32 cmd)
 {
 	void *cmdbuf;
@@ -369,7 +379,7 @@ static int fpc1020_tz_send_locked(struct fpc1020 *fpc1020, u32 group, u32 cmd)
 	fpc1020_put32(cmdbuf, 0, group);
 	fpc1020_put32(cmdbuf, 1, cmd);
 
-	ret = fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked(fpc1020, cmdbuf, FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE);
+	ret = fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked(fpc1020, cmdbuf, FPC1020_TZ_CMDBUF_SIZE);
 	if (!ret) {
 		s32 status = (s32)fpc1020_get32(cmdbuf, 2);
 
